a. Test this UI mock-up. Can you find any problems with this screen?
At first glance, the screen looks like a standard payment form. However, I can see several potential issues.
1. Missing country selection (Critical issue)
Since this is a global SaaS company, I would expect the payment form  should support customers from different countries.
The form has a State or Province dropdown, but there is no option to select a country. It also looks like the form was mainly designed for US addresses.
Customers outside the US might not be able to enter their billing address correctly, which could prevent them from completing the payment.
2. Payment amount is not clear
The payment amount is displayed as 30.00, but there is no currency symbol.
It is also unclear whether this is a one-time payment or a monthly or yearly subscription, and whether taxes are included.
Customers should clearly understand what they are paying for and how much they will be charged.
3. Credit card validation
I would check that the system properly validates credit card numbers, rejects invalid characters, and does not accept expired cards.
I would also expect the system to automatically detect the card type based on the card number instead of relying on the customer to select it manually.
4. Missing CVV field
There is no CVV field in the form.
This does not necessarily mean the payment will fail, since some payment providers support transactions without CVV. However, I would check whether it is required by the payment provider and whether its absence affects transaction approval rates.
5. Potential security issues
Since the form handles sensitive payment information, I would verify that:
- All communication is secured using HTTPS.
- Credit card information is not exposed in logs or unnecessarily stored in the browser.
- Payments are handled through a PCI DSS-compliant payment provider.
- Input validation is performed on the server side as well as the client side.
- The payment amount cannot be manipulated by modifying the request sent from the browser.
These are potential risks to investigate, not confirmed security issues based on the mock-up alone.
6. Usability issues
There are several things I would improve:
- The card number field requires users to enter numbers without spaces or dashes. It would be more user-friendly to accept formatted input and remove the separators automatically.
- The Middle Initial field may not be relevant to customers in many countries.
- The second address line does not have a clear label.
- It is unclear how validation errors are displayed.
- I would also test mobile responsiveness, keyboard navigation, and screen reader accessibility.
7. Performance and error handling
I would test what happens when the user clicks Continue and the server responds slowly or fails.
The system should show a loading indicator and prevent users from submitting the payment multiple times.
If a payment fails, the user should receive a clear error message and be able to retry without entering all non-sensitive information again.
